XPost Internals
===============

How the interpreter works, from the memory file up: which module holds
what, how the modules layer, the virtual memory and the objects in it,
the operator dispatch, the devices, the recorded page and the band
loop.

doc/ROOTS is the companion file: the garbage collector's root set,
bank by bank.


Running out of the source tree
------------------------------

Xpost finds the PostScript files it boots from by looking for init.ps,
in this order (xpost_interpreter.c:xpost_interpreter_data_dir):

    $XPOST_DATA_DIR
    the directory worked out from the shared library's own location
    the source tree's data directory, as recorded at configure time
    the configured PACKAGE_DATA_DIR
    data, ../data, ../../data, relative to the working directory

The second and third of those are what make a build tree
self-sufficient. The second is computed in xpost_main.c from where the
library was loaded: an installed one looks beside itself for
../share/xpost, and where that is not there -- an uninstalled build --
it tries ../../../data instead, which is the source tree's own data
directory from either build/src/lib or src/lib/.libs. That walk supposes
the build directory sits inside the tree; the third candidate is the
same directory named absolutely, compiled in when the build was
configured, and holds for a build directory anywhere. So an interpreter
built and not installed finds the tree's PostScript files from any
working directory, and a change to one of them takes effect without
installing anything. A shell alias is enough to keep one to hand:

    alias xpost="$PWD/build/src/bin/xpost"

The same fallback is a trap for anything that reads the tree
programmatically. A guard handed the wrong source root does not fail --
it finds a working tree by one of those routes and reports a perfectly
true result about a tree the caller did not mean. tests/guard-paths.sh
exists for that reason; see doc/CONTRIBUTING.md.


