Virtual Memory
--------------

  -- Xpost_Memory_File --

At the heart is the "xpost_memory" module: the memory-file and memory-tables.
Every Xpost_Memory_File starts with an Xpost_Memory_Table.
Every Xpost_Memory_Table lives in an Xpost_Memory_File.
An Xpost_Memory_File is a raw memory space holding one large region,
sub-allocated with a segmented index, the Xpost_Memory_Table.

The region is obtained in one of three ways, chosen by what the host offers.
What is wanted is a region that grows without moving, since a grow that copies
pays for the whole file each time it doubles:

    a mapping extended in place	    mmap with mremap
    a reservation committed by	    mmap without mremap, and Win64
      the piece
    the host allocator		    a host with neither

The middle arrangement reserves the whole range an offset can address, without
being charged for it, and commits more of it as the file fills. The base never
moves and no copy is made, which is what the first gets from mremap. It is also
the only difference that matters to a program: in the first two the file owns
the pages beneath it, and in the third it borrows them from the allocator, so
only the first two can give storage back (see Returning storage, below).

The configure summary reports which arrangement a build has as "memory
backing". `-Dmmap=false` forces the allocator, and `-Dmremap=false` forces the
reservation on a host that could extend a mapping, which is how that
arrangement is exercised where it would not otherwise be reached.

A datum stored in memory is here alternately called an "entity"
(when referring to the abstract data type, ie. the object data itself,
the raw bytes) or an "entry" (when referring to the allocation metadata
in the table (its allocated size, the address of the data).

An execution context has two Memory Files: one for global VM, one for local VM.
Allocations are made into the current memory file selected by a
parameter in the context, which may be altered with the `bool
setglobal` operator. Global allocations may not contain local objects.
Local may contain global. Remember, local memory is more perishable.
It is subject to save and restore. So this rule prevents dangling
links in global memory.

A context is forked by the `fork` operator (xpost_op_context.c), and there
is one kind of fork: it shares both memory files and gives the new
context stacks of its own. Fresh memory files are not an option at this
layer, which builds none of the special entities -- the name stacks and
trees, the operator table -- that a context's collection floor already
says exist; those are built by the interpreter above it
(xpost_context.c:xpost_context_fork3 says the same at more length).
These are execution contexts within one interpreter, held in that
interpreter's context table; they are not multiple interpreters. A
process holds one interpreter instance, which is what xpost_create
returns a context of and xpost_destroy ends.

  -- Xpost_Memory_Table --

At address 0 of the Xpost_Memory_File is an Xpost_Memory_Table.
An Xpost_Memory_Table is one segment of a chain of address tables which hold
the allocation info (size, flags) and the "pointer" to the data
itself. All VM "pointers" are integer byte-offsets from the vm
base pointer.

The Memory Table struct contains allocation slots and a counter. A simple linear
allocator increases the counter to dole-out slots in the table. The counter only
ever rises, so a slot that stops being used is recovered rather than skipped: a
slot whose storage is still there goes on a free list and is handed out again
with its storage, and one whose storage has gone -- which is what closing the
arena up leaves behind -- goes on a list of its own and is handed out with fresh
storage. Without the second of those a job that fragments and closes up
repeatedly would spend the whole range of slot numbers without spending any
memory.

An index into the slots of the memory table is called an "ent". The
slot contains the raw address of the allocation, a field to hold
accounting data (marks for gc, upper+lower save-levels), the length of
the allocation, and a type field which may hold an object tag as a
hint to the garbage collector. This allows the garbage collector to
close files.

  -- Xpost_Memory_Table[0] Special Entities --

The first several entries of Xpost_Memory_Table[0] are "special entities".
All Xpost_Memory_File have (in the Xpost_Memory_Table[0]) a save stack (VS), a
free-list (FREE) and a context-list (CTXLIST) which contains `cid`s
of all contexts sharing that particular Xpost_Memory_File. This was devised
to help encapsulate the context's global state within the Xpost_Memory_File
construct. Even the operator table OPTAB is a special entity
and lives in global VM, not "global memory".
(In case somebody needs custom operators, it is possible).

Both VMs go on to hold NAMES, NAMET and BOGUSNAME.
NAMES and NAMET are for the Name String stack and Name Tree,
a ternary search tree living in the memory file like everything
else.

Bogusname is an allocated name string corresponding to the
Not-Found result. So zero means No and asking what No means
is Bogus.

OPTAB is unique to global memories.

        Special Entities
        ----------------
           context
    (Xpost_Memory_File)gl   (Xpost_Memory_File)lo
      Xpost_Memory_Table        Xpost_Memory_Table
        FREE        FREE
        VS          VS
        CTXLIST     CTXLIST
        NAMES       NAMES
        NAMET       NAMET
        BOGUSNAME   BOGUSNAME
        OPTAB       ----
        ----        live-entries (gc) ctx->lo->start
        live-entries (gc) ctx->gl->start

The special entities in the Xpost_Memory_Table facilitate bookkeeping of the
Xpost_Memory_Table themselves (FREE, VS), locating associated contexts (CTXLIST),
and internal context data (possibly shared) (NAMES, NAMET, OPTAB).
If everything works right, the presence of BOGUSNAME should have
no visible effect to the user.

The enumeration is in src/lib/xpost_memory.h, spelt
XPOST_MEMORY_TABLE_SPECIAL_FREE and so on, with
XPOST_MEMORY_COLLECT_START_LOCAL and ..._GLOBAL naming the first entry
past the special ones in each bank.

  -- Addresses, and the coding rule --

Addresses to memory occur in several definite forms, which represent several
levels of "crackng the nut". Since the base pointer to memory, in either of the malloc
or mmap configurations, will be an unsigned char *, a raw memory address is an
integer which indexes the memory array, ie. a byte-offset.
Since the memory may move whenever it must realloc(), this "relative" pointer
is the safest analogue to an "absolute" pointer into the memory space.

    The practical upshot is this Coding Rule for Xpost source code,
    including device operator functions:

	Do Not hold a pointer while allocating!

    Don't do it. You can get real `char*`s into memory if you want.
    It's not that difficult. The xpost_dev_* files do this. But if
    you want to keep using it, you cannot create new composite objects.
    No new arrays, strings, dicts, or growing a dict past its maxlength.
    No pushing onto stacks past their current level. Because stacks are
    segmented, too, and live in the same malloc() or mmap() block.
    No new names, because names have associated strings AND live on a stack.
    There are lines in the source commented "//recalc" that show how
    to refresh a pointer after allocating, if you really need to do that.

    Allocating is not the only thing that invalidates one. Closing the
    arena up moves the bytes an entity holds, so every pointer derived
    from an entity's recorded address is stale afterwards however it was
    obtained. That is why the rearrangement is done in the outermost loop
    of the interpreter and nowhere else: an operator runs underneath
    machinery holding such pointers, and so does every nested call running
    a procedure, each holding the storage of the array it is running.
    Only the outermost loop has none of them below it, and
    tests/check-compaction-safe-point.sh holds the code to that.


Objects
-------

The postscript object contains 4 fields if it's a composite object which must
"point" to memory.

    tag
    size
    ent
    offset

In the 32bit configuration, these are 16-bits each. In the LARGE_OBJECT
configuration, these are 32-bits each.

The tag contains a type bitfield which identifies the kind of data (string, array,
dict, file), and the ent field (+ a few bits from the tag) is the index into
the memory tables. Another bit in the tag indicates whether this ent refers
to the global memory or local memory. Having the offset and size fields in the
object makes getinterval very cheap. Simple objects typically use a combined
ent+offset field for the payload value. So an integer object has only three
fields

     16bit tag
     16bit pad
     32bit val

Several object types with simple needs use the marktype object struct which has
three fields

     16bit tag
     16bit pad0
     32bit unsigned padw

Operator objects use .mark.padw to hold the opcode. Name objects use .mark.padw
to hold the Name Stack index.

These objects may live in memory as components of arrays and dicts (stringtype objects
cannot contain other objects; there is no way to directly access an object's
representation as a string from the Postscript level). Or they may live on stacks,
which are also in memory.

Names are implemented with a ternary search tree that maps strings to integers.
The integer is stored in the nametype object in a combined ent+offset field.
It indexes a "name string stack" which contains postscript string objects
which contain the reverse mappings. One caveat: the search tree algorithm 
assumes the string is nul-terminated. A nul byte is always added before searching
(cheaply, with alloca()) but this does mean that you cannot embed nuls in names,
even if you manually convert using `string cvn`.

Dictionaries are implemented as an open hash with N+1 slots to enable terminate-on-
null in the searching.

Names are persistant through the execution lifetime of the interpreter,
but arrays and dictionaries are subject to garbage collection and to explicit
discarding by `restore`.

Composite object constructors return objects which are marked 'executable'.
It would be wise to explicitly specify the executable/literal attribute when
creating the object. So, instead of this:

    Xpost_Object arr = xpost_array_cons(ctx, 12);

do this:

    Xpost_Object arr = xpost_object_cvx(xpost_array_cons(ctx, 12));

or, to make a non-executable object:

    Xpost_Object arr = xpost_object_cvlit(xpost_array_cons(ctx, 12));

Stacks
------

Patterned after the model of the memory-table chain is the
"xpost_stack" module: the segmented stack.

Stacks live in local memory and grow by the segment. A stack is a chain of
segments, each one an entity with a row of its own, and each naming its
neighbours by entity number; the root segment's backward link names the segment
holding the topmost value, so reaching the top costs no walk. Segments are not
given up when a stack shrinks -- one emptied is left chained above the top for
the next push to move back into.

And making use of both of these is the "xpost_save" module:
the *virtual* part of the virtual-memory, the save/restore stacks
which live in the memory file.

These three modules (memory, stack, & save) are considered the "core"
of the whole thing.

HOLD is a stack to hold arguments popped from the
operand stack before being passed to operator functions. This
facilitates argument passing by cracking the hold stack array
(since the hold stack should be at most one segment. The desired
objects from the operand stack may span a segment boundary, but
in the hold stack, they are rooted (so we can use the stack as an array,
so we can select (switch) a function call based on the number of
objects).

HOLD is referenced directly in the context struct, as are all of
the execution stacks. They cannot be special entities of a memory
file: the vm may be shared between two contexts, so the stacks are
per-context data, not per-memory-file data.


Composite Objects
-----------------

Array, String and Dictionary functions come in 2 flavors,
a "raw" version which expects to have the Xpost_Memory_File specified, and
a "friendly" version which interprets the BANK field in the tag
to determine whether to look in the local or global VM.

The raw one carries a _memory suffix and the friendly one does not:

    xpost_array_get_memory(mem, a, i)   raw
    xpost_array_get(ctx, a, i)          banked

and the same pairing for _put, for the constructors, and for the string
and dictionary equivalents.


Save and Restore
----------------

The save stack holds save-records with two entities, the source and the
copy. restore merely has to exchange the addresses of the two entities.
All array and dictionary write operations ensure that the memory is
saved at the current level before writing.
"True" copy-on-write and no pointer-chasing.

Arrays ("xpost_array") and dictionaries ("xpost_dict") are automatically
copied before writing if the save-level has risen, giving cheap 'save's.
'restore' has an easy job of popping the save-stack and exchanging
the addresses in the memory-tables: cheap 'restore's.

PLRM 3.7.3 exempts global virtual memory from save and restore: what a
program puts there survives a restore that ends the save it was made in.
So global VM is reclaimed by collection alone, which is why the collector
covers both banks.


Garbage Collection
------------------

The garbage collector module ("xpost_garbage") implements a simple (?)
mark-sweep algorithm. It operates on a per-Xpost_Memory_File basis. Local VM is
collected, and so is global VM. Marking covers the banks the sweep will read,
no more. A collection of both banks walks across the boundary -- an object in
one may be named from the other. A collection of one bank stays in it and
stops at any reference that leaves it: what lies beyond is not swept, and
local may name global freely, so crossing would walk the whole global graph
to protect storage the sweep never touches. The references the other way are
enumerable -- a global container may hold no reference into local memory
(PLRM 3.7.2) except the local dictionaries systemdict names through
sanctioned windows -- and those are marked as anchors either way;
tests/check-global-local-anchors.sh holds the register of windows to the
code and each anchor to survival of a local-only collection. A bank is swept
only by a collection that marked it. All contexts sharing an
Xpost_Memory_File must be traversed in the /mark/ phase. PLRM 8.2's vmreclaim
chooses which banks an immediate collection reclaims, and which the
automatic ones do.

The root set is doc/ROOTS, thing by thing, with the bank each lives in.

The algorithm is kept very simple, because the memory
interface itself is complicated. Even conceptually simple operations
like copying a pointer from the free list get involved, eg.

    memcpy(mem->base+z, mem->base+adrent(mem,e), sizeof(unsigned));

This line copies the "pointer" (unsigned) value indicated by the
entity e into the memory "pointed to" by the "pointer" z.

A collection is asked for when the bytes allocated since the last one
exceed a threshold (XPOST_GARBAGE_COLLECTION_THRESHOLD,
src/lib/xpost_free.h, which the environment variable XPOST_GC_THRESHOLD
overrides). The allocator only marks a collection pending; it is taken
at the interpreter's safe point between operators.

The free list is a chain of entity numbers, bucketed by size class. The
bucket heads live in the data area of the FREE special entity, and each
entity on a list names the next through the `nextfree` field of its own
row in the memory table; an ent number of zero terminates a list. The
storage a freed entity stands for holds nothing the allocator reads,
which is what lets that storage be treated as meaningless once released:
a write into it through a stale reference spoils no link, a sanitizer
can be told the whole extent is closed rather than all but its first
word, and a pass that closes the arena up can slide a live entity over
it without reading it first. An allocation examines at most
XPOST_FREE_SCAN_LIMIT entries in any one bucket before taking a larger
entry from a higher bucket, or a fresh one.


Returning storage
-----------------

Storage a collection recovers goes on a free list, where a later allocation of a
size it suits takes it. The reserved address range does not shrink -- an offset
must go on being able to name any byte the arena could hold -- but the committed
pages beneath it are handed back, so a server's resident footprint follows what a
job needs and not the largest any job once reached. Two paths return them: an
immediate `vmreclaim`, which closes the arena up and hands back the run it
gathers (below), and the job boundary, which reverts a job's whole arena and
hands back everything above the baseline cursor.

A program asking for a collection with a positive `vmreclaim` operand gets more
than a collection. The arena is closed up: the live entities are sorted by
address and slid down over the free blocks between them, so the free storage
gathers into one run at the top, and the cursor is left where the live storage
ends. The pages under that run go back to the system in a single call.

This is writable at all only because every block in the arena carries a row in
the memory table. A pass that closes the arena up walks the table, and a block
with no row could not be seen -- an entity slid down would be moved on top of
it. The one field that says where an entity's bytes are is the row's, so moving
an entity is rewriting that field as the bytes move; nothing else in virtual
memory records a place.

The blocks slid over stop existing. Their storage is inside what the live
entities now occupy, so the lists that named them are emptied and their rows are
handed back for their numbers to be issued again. A bank that has just been
closed up therefore reports nothing on its free lists: what a collection would
have left there lies above the cursor instead.

Closing the arena up is done between operator executions, in the outermost loop
of the interpreter, and not by the operator that asks for it. It moves the bytes
under every pointer derived from an entity's recorded address, and an operator
runs underneath machinery that holds such pointers -- as does every nested call
running a procedure, each holding the storage of the array it is running. Only
the outermost loop has none of those below it. A reclaim asked for deep inside a
procedure waits until the procedure has finished, which is no loss: a program
asks for a reclaim, not for a moment.

Because the cursor comes back, the used figure `vmstatus` reports is where the
live storage ends rather than the high-water mark of the job. The account of a
bank is then maximum less used. Collections that run of their own accord do not
close the arena up, and leave the storage where it is.

It is a trade rather than a saving: the cost is the page faults to write that
storage again, paid by whichever later allocation takes it, and the work of
moving every live byte. A program asking for a reclaim is saying it would rather
pay that than hold the memory.

Each host is asked for it by a different call, and all three give the storage up
while leaving the range writable, so nothing has to be asked for before the
range is used again:

    Linux	    madvise MADV_DONTNEED
    other POSIX	    the range re-mapped in place, MAP_FIXED
    Windows	    DiscardVirtualMemory, from Windows 8.1

The middle one is plain POSIX and serves Linux too, at about three times the
cost of the call above it; Linux uses the cheaper one, and XPOST_RETURN_REMAPS
in the environment asks for the portable route anyway, which is how it is
exercised on a host that would not otherwise reach it.

Storage is given up a page at a time, which is not the unit a memory file is
sized in: on Windows the region is handed out in allocation-granularity units,
sixteen times the page. The two are separate numbers, xpost_memory_page_size and
xpost_memory_return_grain.


Tunable parameters
------------------

The stack data structure was designed as a "chain", ie. a linked-list of
segments of a larger, "virtual" array, but using "addresses" in the sense
described above, rather than C pointers.  The size of each segment may be
changed by adjusting the constant in the header file.

    XPOST_STACK_SEGMENT_SIZE            src/lib/xpost_stack.h

The free list's shape is set by two constants:

    XPOST_FREE_NBUCKETS                 src/lib/xpost_free.h
    XPOST_FREE_SCAN_LIMIT               src/lib/xpost_free.h

the number of size-class buckets, and how many entries an allocation
may examine in any one of them.

The garbage collector's pacing is

    XPOST_GARBAGE_COLLECTION_THRESHOLD  src/lib/xpost_free.h

which measures bytes allocated between collections, and which the
environment variable XPOST_GC_THRESHOLD overrides.


Matrices
--------

For ease of implementation, the postscript matrix [a b c d e f]
is treated internally as the transpose of the diagrams in the PLRM.
This produces identical results, while permitting the more C-friendly
syntax of right-multiplying matrices. Whereas Postscript's model of
left-multiplying is more appropriate for its reverse-polish syntax.


Logging
-------

The logging system, implemented by Vincent Torri, is controlled by
the environment variable XPOST_LOG_LEVEL with an integer code,
meaning:

    XPOST_LOG_LEVEL_ERR  0
    XPOST_LOG_LEVEL_WARN 1
    XPOST_LOG_LEVEL_INFO 2
    XPOST_LOG_LEVEL_DBG  3

For debugging, the DBG level, of course is very helpful. But it
may be necessary to redirect output to an external file, possibly
with `tee`. There is a lot of output.


Operator Handling
-----------------

An operator object uses a combined ent+offset field to carry an index
into the operator table, which lives in global vm. The optab is
populated at init() by `struct oper` objects: a table entry holds an
array of type signatures with a function pointer beside each. The
signatures are matched against the objects on the operand stack, and
the function belonging to the first that matches is called with those
objects as ordinary C arguments -- which is what the HOLD stack above
is for, and is done by a loop that abuses the `switch` statement and
unprototyped function pointers. It combines the type check, the
stack-underflow check and a polymorphic variadic dispatch in one pass.

At the same time the optab is populated, the systemdict is populated
with the operator's name and a postscript-level operator object. Each
xpost_op_*.c file implements a different functional group of
operators.

Output devices implement operator functions in much the same
way internal operators do, but installing the operator object
into the device dictionary instead of systemdict.


Bringing it all together
------------------------

The itp structure holds the context table ctab, and the Xpost_Memory_File
tables ltab and gtab. Special entry CTXLIST lists all contexts associated
with an Xpost_Memory_File.

So the garbage collector's root-set for any given Xpost_Memory_File is the VS
(the save stack) (only one for each Xpost_Memory_File, shared contexts are blocked
during save/restore sections), and the stacks from each associated context.

Context IDs, `cid`s, are generated sequentially, starting from 1.
They are designed so that `(cid - 1) % MAXCONTEXT` will yield the
index in the ctab of the context. So the allocator increments
until this formula yields an empty slot. This lets us re-use slots
while still maintaining unique IDs for all processes in the history.

Names are banked like composites, and for the same reason the composites
are. PLRM 3.7.2 says only composite objects occupy VM and a global object
may not reference a local one; a name is a simple object, but the string
it is spelt with and the tree it is found through are composites in one
bank or the other. The system names go in the global bank, since the
optab lives there and operator names have to be reachable in the same
Xpost_Memory_File; a name a program makes is allocated wherever the
allocation mode says, like anything else.


Devices
-------

A Device is much like systemdict: it is a dictionary of name->operator
pairs. The raster classes are implemented entirely in postscript over
one shared method suite (.rasterproto, specialised per format by
.makerasterclass, data/image.ps), and a class can be sub-classed,
overriding as many operators as desired, in order to capture graphical
output or optimize graphical throughput.

Here is the 8-bit grayscale class, .xpost_PGMIMAGE (data/pgmimage.ps).
Colour differs only in the number of `val` objects passed on the stack
to each procedure. It uses the full range 0-255 of values and does not
calculate the actual maximum value in the data before transmitting;
MaxVal is always 255. .xpost_PGMIMAGE is the Class dictionary, IMAGE an
instance returned by Create:

           width height .xpost_PGMIMAGE  Create  ->  IMAGE
                   val x y IMAGE  PutPix  ->  -
                       x y IMAGE  GetPix  ->  val
           val x1 y1 x2 y2 IMAGE  DrawLine  ->  -
      val x y width height IMAGE  DrawRect  ->  -
      val x y width height IMAGE  FillRect  ->  -
               val polygon IMAGE  FillPoly  ->  -
                           IMAGE  Emit  ->  -
                           IMAGE  Destroy  ->  -

DrawLine calls PutPix in a Bresenham-DDA line including endpoints;
FillRect and FillPoly call DrawLine for each scanline. eg:

    PS> /dev 40 20 newPGMIMAGEdevice def
    PS> 55 1 1 38 17 dev dup /DrawLine get exec
    PS> 77 9 14 3 3 dev dup /FillRect get exec
    PS> dev dup /Emit get exec

Create builds the raster as /ImgData, an array with one row per
scanline, priced before any of it is built. For the minimal work to
get the graphics data at the C level, you can override the /Emit
member of a derived class and read ImgData. Another option is to
override the PutPix method: the C function will receive a call with
pixel values for each pixel. A device that keeps a buffer of its own
instead of ImgData must override every inherited method that reads
ImgData.

The xcb device (and similarly for the win32 device, depending on
availability detected at configure time) is hard-patched into the
initialization procedures and installs two operators into systemdict:
loadxcbdevice() and loadxcbdevicecont(). The loading is divided into
two C functions, so we can call into postscript and then back again.
We use postscript to handle invoking the base class /Create method,
and then modifying the Device object and re-using that as a new class,
the xcbdevice class (src/lib/xpost_dev_xcb.c).

loadxcbdevicecont() is responsible for overriding the Postscript
procedures with new operator functions.

The Create operator is also divided into _create() and _create_cont().
Again, this is so it may call the base class's create method whether
it is a postscript procedure or a C function (postscript operator).

The xpost_dev_xcb.c:_create_cont() operator function actually creates
the window and maps it. Then for speed, it overrides the drawing
methods: PutPix, DrawLine, FillRect, and FillPoly.

A device may (but is not required to) implement a /Flush method which
should flush any buffered drawing operations and syncronize the output
with the execution of the postscript program.

Only a single running window device is supported, as it requires
constant attention by the main loop of the interpreter in the form of
the event-handler function.


The Raster device
-----------------

The Raster device is intended for use when xpost is a library component
of another program. It can make use of configuration options from
the call to xpost_create() to return the image buffer to the calling
program, as well as controlling the behavior of the `showpage`
operator. It can return an image buffer in several raw formats
selected by these device strings:
"raster:bgr", "raster:bgra", "raster:rgb", "raster:argb".
Those four are the whole of what the selection may carry: a word
outside them is refused as the interpreter is created, naming what
was given and the four, and a selection naming no format gets rgb.

For interactive use, showpage flushes the drawing to the window
and reads a line from the keyboard (waits for the Enter key).
For library use, it may instruct showpage to `quit` the interpreter
which will return control back to the caller. 
The caller will then have access to the raster image data.

The postscript program is then suspended at the point just before
`showpage` returns. The suspended program may be continued by the
caller (possibly to produce subsequent pages) by calling xpost_run
again with the instruction to Resume.

For single-page postscript programs, and depending upon the
details of the chosen device's behavior, it may be simpler
to call xpost_run with the Showpage semantic to not-pause
rather than the early return described above.  The postscript
program will execute to completion and the interpreter will
clean-up and exit, particularly calling the device's Destroy
function which may be necessary for it to finalize its actions
(such as writing files with the PNG device).


The Record
----------

A raster costs width times height whatever is painted on it. A device whose
page may arrive a band at a time is given one run of rows after another
instead, which bounds it to width times the band. The page's marks are held
in place of it: a page is not finished until `showpage`, marks arrive in any
order and overpaint freely, so no row can be called complete early and
streamed out.

The store is xpost_record.c; xpost_dev_record.c is a device which implements
the driver contract and, instead of painting, writes down what it was asked
to paint; data/recorddev.ps is the class. A record is outside virtual memory
and holds no Xpost_Object, so `restore` does not reach it and `vmstatus` does
not count it (doc/ROOTS).

The recorder declares exactly the five marking methods it records -- PutPix,
BlendPix, DrawLine, FillRect, FillPoly -- and no other optional method.
FillPath, DrawRect and Erase are then resolved above the device into those
five, so a record holds no clip, no outline and no page reset of its own.
Declaring one of them would put marks beyond the record's reach and every
page using them would replay short. tests/check-device-skeleton.sh holds a
recorder to declaring no more than the five.

An entry is one of nine kinds (Xpost_Record_Kind, xpost_record.h):

    PUTPIX    <colour> x y
    BLENDPIX  <colour> coverage x y
    DRAWLINE  <colour> x1 y1 x2 y2
    FILLRECT  <colour> x y w h
    FILLPOLY  <colour> n, then n pairs
    IMAGE     which picture, placed by its own transform
    SCREEN    which threshold cell
    GLYPH     which coverage mask, then x y
    PLACE     which drawing, then dx dy

Every entry carries the rows it can reach. That range must be a conservative
superset of them: a visit too many costs time, a mark judged out of a band it
touches is absent from the page. A replay takes a run of rows and visits only
the entries whose range meets it.

The four kinds that are bulk pixels rather than geometry follow one rule: a
bitmap produced once is held once and re-emitted per band. So a picture is
one entry naming its samples, the transform that places them and the colour
setup that decodes them; a glyph is one entry naming a coverage mask, 0 for a
pixel not painted and 255 for the whole of one. Masks are shared on their
bytes rather than on a name, and are the record's own copies -- the cache of
rendered masks is bounded and may drop an entry a record still names. A
tiling pattern is not covered by the rule: its cells are painted by running
the paint procedure once per cell (.patterntile, data/pattern.ps), so a
pattern writes the cell's whole drawing down once for every cell.

A screen entry is not a mark. It is played in the order it was made, is
exempt from the row filter a band replay applies, and is skipped by
everything that asks about ink -- state set before a band governs that band
wherever on the page it was set. It survives the page boundary: the screen in
force is written down as the first entry of the page beginning.

A recorder is not told when a page ends. `erasepage` reaches it as a
rectangle covering the page, a page is cleared as it starts, and that is the
whole of how a page boundary arrives; the record gives up what it held from
before such a rectangle. Reading the boundary off the marks is what keeps a
page put out twice right, `copypage` transmitting a page without erasing it
(PLRM 8.2).

A record is fixed to the resolution it was made at. Its coordinates are
device space, the clip having been resolved into them above the device, so
replaying at another scale gives a scaled copy of the same pixels rather than
a re-rendering.


Forms
-----

PLRM 4.7 permits an interpreter to keep a form's graphical output and
substitute it for a later execution. What is kept here is a record of the
marks rather than a raster of them, so one execution serves every use under
one transformation wherever it falls, and a use is one PLACE entry rather
than a copy. Nesting is resolved when the page is painted, so forms inside
forms cost each distinct drawing once at each transformation it stands under
rather than their product. A replay descends a level per placement, bounded
at XPOST_RECORD_NEST (12, xpost_record.h); past that the page is refused with
`limitcheck`, at the placement and at the replay.

A description is captured only where placing the marks is what painting them
would have been (data/init.ps). Nothing may have been cut on the way into the
drawing, which .clipcuts counts, and the marks must lie inside the box the
form declares, which .recordbox reads back; a description failing either is
painted afresh at every use. The capture paints under a clip of the declared
box out to whole pixels and one further, which holds a description painting
outside the box it declared to about that box and leaves one that keeps
inside it uncut.


The band loop
-------------

data/recorddev.ps plays a record into the device the run selected. That
device holds the pixels; the record holds no rows of its own.

A class states whether its page may arrive a band at a time with
/BandedPage, and silence means no. pgm, ppm, pbm, tiff, png and jpeg declare
it. The raster and BGR devices take it back out, the page they hold being an
embedder's and whole by contract; so do the window devices, whose drivers
write the page whole, and every compiled device keeping a raster of its own.
The alpha PNG device takes it back out for a reason of its own: a page
arrives in bands by being recorded and played back, a record writes a
page reset down as a rectangle in the colour it clears to, and the page
this device clears to is transparent rather than any colour -- so the
reset that makes it what it is does not survive the round trip. Its
writer takes a page a band at a time regardless, which is what
tests/run-band-writer-test.sh drives with no record in front of it.
tests/check-device-skeleton.sh holds every class to having said
something, once for each class a driver installs. pbm and tiff are copies
of the grayscale and colour classes and keep the answer they were copied
with, each having earned it: the comments at the head of data/pbmimage.ps
and data/tiffimage.ps say what stood in the way of a banded page in each
format and why it does not.

The declaration and the routing are a pair. .playtargets (data/recorddev.ps)
says which devices a record may be played into, and a device declaring a
banded page and named nowhere there promises an arrival no budget can
give it. tests/check-device-roster.sh holds the two together, asking a
running interpreter what each device's class declares rather than reading
it off a driver's source: a class is built and not spelled, and one
driver body here makes two classes and answers differently for each.

A class states what one row of its raster costs the same way, as /.rowcost,
a pair of the elements a row takes and its bytes. That one a copy must
restate rather than inherit, and the guard holds a derived class to it:
a budget divided by a number nobody restated buys a band of some other
height than the one it paid for.

One raster, moved, rather than one device per band: a raster comes out of the
half of virtual memory a restore does not rewind, and a device the page
machinery has retired stays reachable, so its rows are not given back. A
device per band would end the loop holding the whole page. What moves is the
run of rows (.moveband, data/image.ps), and a run starts as
a raster fresh from Create would, a row still carrying the previous run's ink
showing it wherever this run paints nothing. The same holds a level up: the
raster is kept on the recording device and the page after stands on it, made
again only where it could not stand for the page in hand.

The array of rows stays the height of the page. .blitrow reaches a device's
rows by page row and has no offset to gain, so a band device presents the
whole spine and holds pixels in the band's rows alone; a row of no width is
how a device already says it holds no pixel of that row, which .blitrow, the
pixel accessors and Emit all read. What that costs is one object slot per row
of the page.

A banding Emit is called once per band, with the rows the device holds at
that moment, and once more at the end with none, to say the page is finished.
What a device must keep between bands cannot live in the rows, since those
move: the file and how far down the page it has reached are kept in .bandpage
(data/image.ps), and a device carrying none takes the whole page as before. A
page goes out through three hooks rather than one writer -- .writehead,
.bandsink, .writetail -- and both routes go through the same three, so the
same bytes come out of one statement rather than two that agree.

A band that comes to nothing but the ground is not materialised. The
predicate is not whether anything reaches the band: a recorder declares no
Erase, so clearing the page reaches it as a rectangle covering the page and
every band of every page is reached by a mark. What is asked is whether the
last mark reaching the run is a rectangle, at the colour the page was cleared
to, covering every row of the run and the whole width; anything else and the
band is painted. What a skipped band saves is time and not memory -- the
raster is priced and taken at Create -- and the rows it stands for are laid
down by the next emission that has rows of its own to write.


The budget
----------

.bandbytes (data/recorddev.ps) is 4,000,000 bytes, which is what a run that
names no budget of its own works to. An invocation may name another with
--band-bytes, and .settlebandbudget (data/device.ps) writes the one it named
under that same name as the run's device is made, which is before the first
page is weighed; so .bandbytes is the budget in force either way and every
reading of it is a reading of what this run works to. .bandheight divides it
by the bytes of /.rowcost, and a budget buying less than one row buys one
row. One number answers two questions: a page bands exactly when the budget
buys fewer rows than the page has, so it bands the moment it would need a
second band and never bands into one.

.devicefor (data/recorddev.ps) asks the first of the two before there is a
device, and decides whether a record is made at all. It is asked wherever a
device is made -- .startdevice (data/device.ps) for the device a run opens
with, and again for every page-device request that makes another
(data/init.ps) -- each at the page that device is to hold, and nowhere
between two of them. A page description states its page by declaring it, so
weighing only the page a run opened at would hold every page a job asked for
whole.

At 4,000,000 the ordinary sheets are painted directly: letter is 612 by 792
at three bytes a pixel, which is 1,454,112 bytes, A4 is 1,502,970 and A3 is
3,008,466. The bound above them is untouched -- a 612 by 40000 strip costs
73,440,000 bytes held whole and is held in 2,178 rows of that instead. A run
that names a budget below those figures is what bands an ordinary sheet.

The comparison is a page's raster against the budget, so what holding the
marks instead costs is not in it: neither the record, which .recordcost
reports, nor the objects a replay builds afresh for each band, which nothing
reports. The number was put above the largest ordinary sheet's raster and
rounded up, and nothing has measured where the two costs cross. It is a free
parameter with a reason for its size and no reading behind its value.

.bandsaving is the run's raster less one band of it, and is the one statement
of what banding a page buys.

None of these is a page-device parameter and none may become one: PLRM
Appendix G bars `banddevice` from a page description, so a program may not
choose how many times its marks are played. What chooses is the invocation.
Being told is the other half, and is allowed: currentsystemparams reports the
budget as MaxBandBytes and the rows it bought on the device now being painted
into as CurBandHeight, which is 0 where that device holds the page whole.
setsystemparams recognises no parameter at all, so neither can be written
back.


The rows an imaging bounding box leaves
---------------------------------------

PLRM 6.2's /ImagingBBox is four numbers in the default user coordinate system
by which a program asserts it paints no marks outside them, and marks that do
fall outside "may or may not be rendered on the output medium". It is not a
clip: the page is the size /PageSize named, the default clipping path is
still the whole of it, and default user space lands where it did.

A device holding its page as rows can drop whole rows and cannot hold half of
one, so what a box buys is the rows above its top edge and below its bottom
edge. .imagingrows (data/device.ps) computes the run once and leaves it in
graphicsdict /.rasterband for the device about to be made. A record takes the
run at its own Create and keeps it, the page device having put the run about
for one device and taken it away again; the raster it plays into stands on
the run, and the band loop goes over the run rather than over the page.

Marks outside the run are dropped, on both routes. It is the only one of the
two answers PLRM permits that both routes can give: the rows outside the run
do not exist on the device holding its page directly. The route is weighed on
the rows a device would hold rather than on the page's height, so a box that
brings the raster under the budget hands the page to the device directly.


Where a retained page's marks are kept
--------------------------------------

A record is held in memory while it is worth less than the raster banding the
page saves (.bandsaving), and goes into a scratch file past that. What the
run asked for is SPILL_AUTO, SPILL_NEVER or SPILL_ALWAYS; where the page in
hand has its marks is SPILT_MEMORY, SPILT_FILE or SPILT_REFUSED.
currentsystemparams reports the pair as RecordSpill and CurRecordSpill, and
they are two names because they can differ: a run that asked to be weighed,
on a machine that will take no scratch file, reports auto and refused.

Under auto the record is weighed every RECORD_WEIGH_EVERY (64) marks, and at
any entry large enough to carry it past the threshold on its own -- a
picture, a coverage mask, a polygon of more than 64 operands. Asking is O(1):
the count of blocks the allocator keeps a header beside is maintained as they
are taken and given up, rather than by walking the masks, the screens and
every table of every picture.

A record saving nothing is never spilled under auto. It has no raster to be
worth more than: its page is held whole, either because the budget covers it
or because the run asked for a band at a page that needs none. A drawing
captured for a page is weighed by the number the page is weighed by.

The file (xpost_spill.c) has no name. It is created and unlinked in the same
breath on POSIX and opened delete-on-close on Windows, so nothing else can
open it and it goes when the handle goes however the process ends; a process
killed hard on Windows can leave it, which is the one place the two platforms
differ. It is not a file in the sense the language means: it is not reached
through the file machinery and the path sandbox does not answer for it, there
being no path to confine. It cannot be had from xpost_mkstemp -- the POSIX
form frees the path it built, so a caller is never told the name and has
nothing to unlink, and the Win32 form opens without FILE_SHARE_DELETE, which
Windows will not unlink.

One stream, in the order the marks were made, and one write buffer for the
page rather than one per band: _SPILL_BUFFER is 64 KiB written and
_SPILL_WINDOW 64 KiB read. Order is what a record is for, so the file keeps
it and a run of rows is chosen by reading past what it does not meet. A chain
per band would cost that order -- a band covering several of them reads them
one after another, so a mark spanning two is played out of order with a mark
inside one -- and would need a write buffer per band. What one stream costs
is a pass over the file per band, twice, once to find whether the band comes
to nothing but the ground and once to play it, so a shallower band is paid in
reading rather than in memory.

Everything the record holds goes into the file: the marks and their values, a
picture's samples and its mask bits, every coverage mask's bytes, every
threshold cell. What stays resident is the two buffers, one small entry per
distinct picture, glyph, screen and placed drawing, and the tables a picture
is decoded through. A picture is read back one sample run at a time. A
coverage mask is read back whole and held while it is painted, a mask being
painted a pixel at a time with the interpreter running between pixels, so a
mask the height of a page is the bound on what a spilled record holds.

Whether a spill file can be made is settled when the record device is made,
by making one and writing to it: a permissions check would answer yes for a
directory on a full filesystem, for one a system-call filter refuses, and for
one mounted read-only under the caller. Under never nothing reaches the
scratch directory at all, not even to look. Nothing else selects never -- a
directory that refuses a file does not demote a run to unbounded memory.


Failure, and never a short page
-------------------------------

A page missing a mark looks like a page. So a record refused a mark remembers
it, refuses every mark after it, and refuses every replay; a caller gets
nothing rather than something short.

  A write that fails leaves the record short of a mark. The refusal is
  `ioerror` and not `VMerror`, because a caller told memory ran out when a
  filesystem filled looks in the wrong place.

  A read that comes back short is a file truncated under a descriptor nobody
  else holds. Same sticky refusal, same `ioerror`. The walk that plays a page
  back tells a drawing that ran out apart from one that stopped answering.

  A scratch directory that becomes unwritable after the file is open cannot
  reach the record: the descriptor is open, the name went when the file was
  made, and directory permissions are not consulted again.

  No scratch space when the device is made. Under always the device is
  refused with `ioerror`; under auto the run is told once, naming the
  directory and the reason the system gave, and goes on, and a page that then
  needs to spill holds its marks in memory.


The replay
----------

XPOST_REPLAY_BATCH (512) calls run before the walk hands the interpreter
back, so that a collection can be taken and an interrupt heard. A glyph mask
is the one entry a batch can end in the middle of, and it resumes at the
pixel it stopped at. A method that is a procedure rather than a compiled
operator is played by leaving the call on the stacks and returning, since
what runs a procedure is the interpreter.

The record takes the target's colour space, component count, glyph coverage
and whether it screens as its own declarations, and the driver installs a
marking suite of the target's arity: two suites of entry points in
xpost_dev_record.c, one per space, a method's operand count being fixed when
the class is installed. A record made in one space and played into a device
declaring another would put each value in the place of a different one; a
replay refuses a mismatched space, and nothing a selection can ask for
reaches it.

A picture is not played as a marking call. It goes back through the writer
that wrote it the first time (.blitrow), which resolves per sample and per
device row which run of device columns that sample covers and states the run
to a sink. A device that keeps rows has the bytes stored into the row; one
that keeps a buffer of its own is handed the run as a call to its own
FillRect. The blit dictionary names either rows or dev, and which of them it
names chooses the sink. The fill must be compiled, what runs a procedure
being the interpreter, which is what keeps the devices putting out drawing
instructions rather than pixels on the rectangle-per-sample route. The same
two sinks serve the direct painting route, so the replay's page and the
device's own page are the same page rather than two that agree. The call
costs more than the store: a magnified picture with /Interpolate blends per
device pixel, so its runs are one column each, and it is the expensive case.

A page whose cost is its marks and almost nothing else costs about twice in
bands what it costs painted directly, a band's marks being played in a loop;
played one at a time through the interpreter it came to 2.6.
tests/run-band-replay-cost-test.sh reads the interpreter's own clock after
`showpage` and holds the ratio at 23/10. It is a ratio and not a time,
because a bound on an absolute time would be a statement about the machine
the suite runs on.

Every gate that compares pages compares one page put out in bands against the
same page put out whole and requires them identical byte for byte, at band
heights down to one row. PLRM 7.5 puts scan conversion outside the language,
so nothing outside this interpreter can say what the right byte at a band
edge is; the whole-page render of the same tree is the only oracle there is.


